# Private terms for the public-repo guard — EXAMPLE FILE.
#
# Copy to `.githooks/private-terms.txt` and put your own patterns in it. The
# copy is git-ignored on purpose: it names private things, so it must never be
# committed here. The committed hook reads whichever file happens to exist.
#
# One extended regular expression per line, matched case-insensitively against
# the commit message and the branch name. `#` starts a comment, blank lines are
# ignored.
#
#   cp .githooks/private-terms.example .githooks/private-terms.txt
#
# Then add, one per line:
#   - the issue-tracker key prefix you use, e.g. `myteam-[0-9]+`
#   - the names of your private projects, services and repositories
#   - the paths of private modules that must never be referenced here
#
# CI reads the same patterns from a repository secret, so a clone without this
# file still gets guarded on pull requests.

# Example: an issue tracker with a three-letter prefix.
# [A-Za-z]{3}-[0-9]+

# Example: private project names and internal module paths.
# my-private-service
# (^|[^a-z0-9_-])(billing|validator|dashboard)/
